title: Grammar & Punctuation
subject: English
level: KS3/GCSE
emoji: ✍️
description: Get your commas, apostrophes and word classes right.
---
What is a noun? :: A word that names a person, place, thing or idea.
What is a verb? :: A word that describes an action, event or state.
What is an adjective? :: A word that describes a noun.
What is an adverb? :: A word that describes a verb, adjective or another adverb (often ends in -ly).
What is a pronoun? :: A word that replaces a noun: he, she, it, they.
What is a conjunction? :: A word that joins clauses or words: and, but, because.
What is a preposition? :: A word showing relationship in place or time: on, under, before.
What is a clause? :: A group of words containing a subject and a verb.
What is a main clause? :: A clause that makes sense on its own.
What is a subordinate clause? :: A clause that depends on a main clause to make sense.
When do you use a semicolon? :: To join two closely related main clauses, or to separate items in a complex list.
When do you use a colon? :: To introduce a list, explanation or quotation after a complete clause.
What is the difference between 'its' and 'it's'? :: 'its' shows possession; 'it's' = 'it is' or 'it has'.
What is the difference between 'their', 'there' and 'they're'? :: Their = belonging to them; there = place; they're = they are.
What is the difference between 'your' and 'you're'? :: Your = belonging to you; you're = you are.
Where does the apostrophe go in 'the dogs' bones' (several dogs)? :: After the s: dogs'.
When do you use 'fewer' vs 'less'? :: Fewer for countable items; less for uncountable amounts.
What is the passive voice? :: When the subject receives the action: 'The ball was kicked by Sam.'
What is the active voice? :: When the subject performs the action: 'Sam kicked the ball.'
What is a comma splice? :: Incorrectly joining two main clauses with just a comma.
What is the difference between 'affect' and 'effect'? :: Affect is usually a verb (to influence); effect is usually a noun (a result).
What is a fronted adverbial? :: A word or phrase at the start of a sentence describing the action, followed by a comma.
